WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.977 --> 00:00:14.981
At Phileas Fogg's mansion Passepartout had read and memorized the card listing his master's daily routine.

2
00:00:15.762 --> 00:00:16.462
He knew that Mr.

3
00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:18.663
Fogg would not be back until exactly midnight.

4
00:00:19.263 --> 00:00:23.445
So Passepartout set about keeping himself busy until his services were needed again.

5
00:00:25.045 --> 00:00:28.707
Passepartout had a pleasant face and a well-built, muscular body.

6
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:33.430
He had tumbled brown hair, blue eyes, and a ruddy complexion.

7
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.854
In his early years, Passepartout had been a sort of vagrant and was not at all brash or

8
00:00:38.914 --> 00:00:45.260
snobby. He had already worked in ten English houses, but so far he had failed to find the

9
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.461
peace he was looking for.

10
00:00:47.561 --> 00:00:50.003
He was happy to have finally found a master who suited him.

11
00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:55.008
Back at the Reform Club, shortly after six o'clock that evening, Mr.

12
00:00:55.028 --> 00:00:56.068
Fogg finished his dinner.

13
00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:00.840
Then he joined five other club members for a game of whist.

14
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.602
Mr. Fogg was a regular player, and his evening card game was an unchanging part of his routine.

15
00:01:06.503 --> 00:01:10.965
The men settled down at a table in front of a fire, and the conversation soon turned to

16
00:01:11.005 --> 00:01:11.685
current affairs.

17
00:01:11.705 --> 00:01:14.746
"What about that robbery then?"

18
00:01:15.226 --> 00:01:16.267
asked one player.

19
00:01:16.287 --> 00:01:20.149
"Oh, the bank will lose the money," replied another.

20
00:01:20.169 --> 00:01:24.571
"The Daily Telegraph says that the thief is a gentleman," said Mr.

21
00:01:24.591 --> 00:01:27.352
Fogg as he set down his newspaper to pick up his cards.

22
00:01:28.872 --> 00:01:32.914
"A man that makes off with fifty-five-thousand pounds is no gentleman!"

23
00:01:33.315 --> 00:01:33.875
said another.

24
00:01:33.895 --> 00:01:36.256
"Possibly not," replied Mr.

25
00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:41.719
Fogg, "but he was reported to be a well-dressed gentleman with polished manners and a well-to-do air about

26
00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:46.422
him." The conversation subsided as the men began their game.

27
00:01:47.923 --> 00:01:52.485
When they paused to deal the cards for a second game, the conversation returned to the robbery.

28
00:01:52.505 --> 00:01:57.108
"Where could the 'gentleman,' if that's what we must call him, go?"

29
00:01:57.992 --> 00:01:59.133
exclaimed one player.

30
00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:03.297
"It would be very difficult to flee with all that money," said another.

31
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:06.079
"No country will be safe for him."

32
00:02:06.099 --> 00:02:10.122
"Oh, I'm not sure about that," said another man.

33
00:02:10.142 --> 00:02:12.164
"The world is big enough."

34
00:02:12.184 --> 00:02:15.467
"It was once," said Mr.

35
00:02:15.507 --> 00:02:16.708
Fogg, "but no longer.

36
00:02:17.588 --> 00:02:20.531
It is now possible to go around the world in eighty days."

37
00:02:20.551 --> 00:02:23.313
"That is true," said one man.

38
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:26.356
"Impossible," said another.

39
00:02:27.288 --> 00:02:31.890
"What if the trains stop running in India, or a storm delays a steamer?

40
00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:32.930
What then?"

41
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:38.092
"All accounted for and anticipated in eighty days," stated Mr.

42
00:02:38.132 --> 00:02:39.233
Fogg with confidence.

43
00:02:40.913 --> 00:02:42.134
"I'd like to see you do it!"

44
00:02:42.494 --> 00:02:43.955
said the player sitting opposite him.

45
00:02:43.975 --> 00:02:47.836
"I'd like nothing better than to do it," said Mr.

46
00:02:47.896 --> 00:02:49.277
Fogg, looking at him calmly.

47
00:02:49.297 --> 00:02:51.578
"When?" asked the man.

48
00:02:51.598 --> 00:02:53.999
"At once," said Mr.

49
00:02:54.039 --> 00:02:54.859
Fogg, smiling.

50
00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:58.305
"It's absurd," cried another player.

51
00:02:58.325 --> 00:03:00.006
"Let's get on with the game!"

52
00:03:01.186 --> 00:03:04.748
Suddenly the man who was about to deal put the cards down and spoke up.

53
00:03:04.768 --> 00:03:07.169
"Fair enough, Mr.

54
00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:11.152
Fogg. I will wager four-thousand pounds on it!"

55
00:03:11.172 --> 00:03:15.554
"I will too," said another, looking to the other players.

56
00:03:15.574 --> 00:03:21.637
"And so will I," said another, and then another, until all five men agreed.

57
00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:29.949
Mr. Fogg looked up from his cards and said, "I have a deposit of twenty- thousand pounds at

58
00:03:29.969 --> 00:03:31.949
the bank that I will gladly risk on it."

59
00:03:31.969 --> 00:03:34.970
"Twenty-thousand pounds!

60
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:40.032
Twenty-thousand pounds that you could lose by a single accidental delay?"

61
00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:42.112
asked the man who had bet first.

62
00:03:42.132 --> 00:03:46.073
"The unforeseen does not exist," replied Mr.

63
00:03:46.113 --> 00:03:48.114
Fogg. "But, Mr.

64
00:03:48.254 --> 00:03:49.594
Fogg," said the man.

65
00:03:49.614 --> 00:03:54.696
"Eighty days are only an estimate of the minimum time the journey can take."

66
00:03:55.884 --> 00:03:59.305
"For this journey, that minimum will suffice," said Mr.

67
00:03:59.345 --> 00:04:06.668
Fogg. "And in order not to exceed the eighty days," the man continued, "you'll need to be very

68
00:04:06.768 --> 00:04:11.871
precise when transferring from trains to steamers and from steamers to trains."

69
00:04:11.891 --> 00:04:16.432
"I will be very precise, gentlemen," said Mr.

70
00:04:16.472 --> 00:04:21.274
Fogg. Phileas Fogg's card partners were both thrilled and concerned.

71
00:04:22.535 --> 00:04:24.876
They were thrilled by a bet they felt certain to win.

72
00:04:26.284 --> 00:04:27.545
But they were concerned that Mr.

73
00:04:27.585 --> 00:04:29.706
Fogg was risking a lot of money on a whim.

74
00:04:29.726 --> 00:04:35.728
"If you are joking, we accept the joke and let you off," offered one man.

75
00:04:35.748 --> 00:04:42.692
"A true Englishman doesn't joke when he is talking about something as serious as a wager," replied Mr.

76
00:04:42.732 --> 00:04:50.675
Fogg. "I will bet twenty-thousand pounds against anyone that I can tour the world in eighty days or

77
00:04:50.695 --> 00:04:52.056
less. Do you accept?"

78
00:04:56.116 --> 00:04:56.676
Hello, again!

79
00:04:57.677 --> 00:05:01.178
Gentlemen's clubs are private clubs for men from the English upper class.

80
00:05:01.898 --> 00:05:03.839
In other words they are not for men like me!

81
00:05:04.039 --> 00:05:07.420
Ha-ha! Members use gentleman's clubs almost as second homes.

82
00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:12.221
They go to these clubs to relax, meet their friends, and enjoy delicious meals.

83
00:05:13.422 --> 00:05:16.983
Mr. Fogg is a member of the Reform Club, and he spends most of his days there.

84
00:05:17.723 --> 00:05:19.364
The Reform Club was founded in 1836.

85
00:05:21.468 --> 00:05:26.752
Membership quickly grew to one thousand and the club's governing committee commissioned an architect to design its current

86
00:05:26.772 --> 00:05:29.354
building, located at 104 Pall Mall.

87
00:05:30.315 --> 00:05:32.817
This street actually has quite a few gentlemen's clubs along it.

88
00:05:33.698 --> 00:05:35.519
The Reform Club is a splendid place.

89
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:41.924
Based on the Farnese Palace in Rome, its central hall is encircled by grand pillars and a spacious

90
00:05:42.045 --> 00:05:42.665
upper gallery.

91
00:05:43.165 --> 00:05:47.269
It has a wonderful kitchen, which was designed according to the chef's specifications.

92
00:05:47.989 --> 00:05:51.072
The Reform Club's library contains over fifty-thousand books!

93
00:05:51.864 --> 00:05:57.308
The Reform Club was founded by a political party known as the Radicals, who embraced liberal political ideas.

94
00:05:58.108 --> 00:06:01.551
So besides being a social club, it is also a political meeting place.

95
00:06:02.151 --> 00:06:08.275
Members use the space to share news, hold meetings, plan campaign strategies, and conduct other party business.
